# HyperTIES — served straight off disk, with hyperties.org as the one canonical name.
#
# The reader is prerendered (adapter-static), so there is no application to proxy to:
# 138 HTML files and about 2 MB of assets. Nothing to keep running, nothing to restart,
# nothing to fall over.
#
# The root is a SYMLINK, not a directory. /data/releases/hyperties/current points at one
# release under releases/, and Caddy resolves the link per request, so publishing a release is a
# rename and a rollback is the same rename backwards -- no rebuild, no restart, no window of
# half-old articles. Deploy with apps/ties/scripts/deploy-app.sh.
#
# That path is literal and global: the same string on the host, in the Caddy container, and in
# the deploy script that writes it. Compose mounts all of /data/releases read-only, so another
# app copying this file only has to change the app name in the root line.
#
# Only hyperties.org serves content. Every other name we own redirects to it, so there is
# one URL to cite, one set of links for search engines to consolidate, and no way to read
# the same article at three addresses.
#
# hyperties.com is NOT ours -- it belongs to an unrelated IT recruitment firm on Polish
# hosting. Nothing here can or should touch it.
#
# Each name needs an A record (and AAAA if the VM has IPv6) pointing at this server
# BEFORE first start, or the ACME challenge fails and Caddy backs off and retries.
# As of 2026-09-20 all six names below resolve to the VM.

# The canonical site, deliberately alone in its block so it gets a certificate of its own.
# When names share a block they share one certificate, and a single name failing its ACME
# challenge takes down every name on it. Isolating hyperties.org means the address people
# actually cite cannot be broken by a problem with .net or .link.
hyperties.org {
	root * /data/releases/hyperties/current
	encode zstd gzip

	# Vite writes content-hashed filenames under _app/immutable, so those are safe to
	# cache forever. Everything else is a document that can change on the next deploy.
	#
	# Both matchers are explicit: an unmatched `header` would apply to the immutable paths
	# too, leaving two Cache-Control values set by the same handler.
	@immutable path /_app/immutable/*
	@documents not path /_app/immutable/*
	header @immutable Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
	header @documents Cache-Control "public, max-age=300"

	file_server

	handle_errors {
		respond "{err.status_code} {err.status_text}" {err.status_code}
	}
}

# Everything else we own, folded into one redirect. {uri} preserves the path and query, so
# a deep link to an article survives the hop instead of dumping the reader on the homepage.
# `permanent` is a 301: browsers and search engines cache it, which is the point.
#
# These five share one certificate. That is the acceptable side of the tradeoff above --
# if one of them fails to validate the others stop redirecting, but the canonical name
# keeps serving, and a broken redirect is a smaller outage than a broken site.
hyperties.net, hyperties.link, www.hyperties.org, www.hyperties.net, www.hyperties.link {
	redir https://hyperties.org{uri} permanent
}
